WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.560
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:10.160
Today, we are going to see a lot of physics&nbsp;
simulations with many-many collisions. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:15.120
particular, you will see a lot of beautiful&nbsp;
footage which contains contact between thin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:21.120
shells and rigid bodies. In this simulation&nbsp;
program, at least one of these objects will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:27.200
always be represented as a signed distance field.&nbsp;
This representation is useful because it helps us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.480
rapidly compute whether something&nbsp;
is inside or outside of this object.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:39.440
However, a collision here takes two objects, of&nbsp;
course, so the other object will be represented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:45.200
as a triangle mesh, which is perhaps the&nbsp;
most common way of storing object geometry in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:51.760
computer graphics. However, with this, we have&nbsp;
a problem. Signed distance fields work great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:57.600
triangle meshes also work great for a number&nbsp;
of applications, however, computing where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:04.080
overlap when they collide is still slow and&nbsp;
difficult. If that does not sound bad enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:10.640
it gets even worse than that. How so? Let’s&nbsp;
have a look together. Experiment number one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.440
Let’s try to intersect this cone with this&nbsp;
rubbery sheet using a traditional technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:22.800
and there is only one rule - no poking through&nbsp;
is allowed. Well, guess what just happened. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.440
earlier technique is called point sampling,&nbsp;
and we either have to check too many points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:33.840
in the two geometries against each other,&nbsp;
which takes way too long and still fails, or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:41.200
we skimp on some of them, but then, this happens.&nbsp;
Important contact points go missing. Not good.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.360
Let’s see how this new method does with&nbsp;
this case. Now that’s what I am talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:53.840
about! No poking through anywhere to&nbsp;
be seen. And, let’s have another look.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:04.000
Wait a second. Are you seeing what I am seeing?&nbsp;
Look at this part…after the first collision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.320
the contact points are moving ever so slightly,&nbsp;
many-many times, and the new method is not missing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:17.760
any of them. Then, things get a little out of&nbsp;
hand, and it still works perfectly. Amazing!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.000
I can only imagine how many of these&nbsp;
interactions the previous point sampling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.040
technique would miss, but we won’t know&nbsp;
because it has already failed long ago.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:35.040
Let’s do another one. Experiment number two.&nbsp;
Dragon versus cloth sheet. This is the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:41.440
point sampling method. We now see that it can&nbsp;
find some of the interactions, but many others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.960
go missing, and due to the anomalies, we can’t&nbsp;
continue the animation by pulling the cloth sheet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:54.880
off of the dragon because it is stuck. Let’s see&nbsp;
how the new method fared in this case. Oh yeah!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:05.360
Nothing pokes through, and therefore, now, we can&nbsp;
continue the animation by doing this. Excellent!

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:15.600
Experiment number three! Rope curtain. Point&nbsp;
sampling. Oh no! This is a disaster. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:21.280
hold on to your papers, and marvel at the new&nbsp;
proposed method. Just look at how beautifully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:27.760
we can pull the rope curtain through this fine&nbsp;
geometry. Loving it. So, of course, if you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.800
a seasoned fellow scholar, you probably want&nbsp;
to know, how much computation do we have to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:38.960
with the old and new methods. How much longer do&nbsp;
I have to wait for the new, improved technique?

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:46.640
Let’s have a look together! Each rigid shell here&nbsp;
is a mesh that uses a 129 thousand triangles, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:52.960
the old point sampling method took 15 milliseconds&nbsp;
to compute the collisions, and this time, it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:59.040
done reasonably well. What about the new one?&nbsp;
How much more computation do we have to perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:05.600
to make sure that our simulations are more robust?&nbsp;
Please stop the video and make a guess. I’ll wait.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:14.160
Alright, let’s see…and, the new one does it in&nbsp;
half a millisecond. Half a millisecond. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:21.040
not slower at all, quite the opposite - thirty&nbsp;
times faster. My goodness! Huge congratulations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:27.200
on yet another masterpiece from scientists&nbsp;
at NVIDIA and the University of Copenhagen.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:31.600
While we look at some more results, in case&nbsp;
you are wondering, the authors used NVIDIA’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:37.040
Omniverse platform to create these amazing&nbsp;
rendered worlds. And now, with this new method,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:43.680
we can infuse our physics simulation programs with&nbsp;
a robust, and blazing fast collision detector, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:49.520
I truly can’t wait to see where talented artists&nbsp;
will take these tools. What a time to be alive!

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.840
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

